{"entries":{"chapter-11-study-prevention-and-screening|Chapter 11 Study: Prevention and Screening":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Prevention acts before symptoms but can impose immediate burdens. Individualize using values, overall health, life expectancy, harms and time-to-benefit (p157).":["u3"],"copy|Many prevention trials exclude adults over 75; extrapolation to older or frail patients therefore carries uncertainty (p157).":["u4"],"copy|Consider cancer screening primarily when life expectancy exceeds 10 years, after weighing the particular test's benefits and harms (p157).":["u5"],"copy|Mammography trial meta-analysis: approximately 11 years to prevent 1 breast-cancer death per 1000 women screened (p160).":["u6"],"copy|Fecal occult blood screening: approximately 10 years to prevent 1 colorectal-cancer death per 1000 people; serious complications are also approximately 1 in 1000 (p161).":["u7"],"copy|Flexible sigmoidoscopy has a similar estimated time-to-benefit; the chapter notes that mortality timing for colonoscopy awaits ongoing trials (p161).":["u8"],"copy|Prior cervical screening is central to decisions in older women. Direct time-to-benefit data are absent for those at least 65 (p163).":["u9"],"copy|Low-dose chest CT trials show lung-cancer mortality reduction after 6 years; chest x-ray screening did not provide such benefit (p163).":["u10"],"copy|Treating asymptomatic hypertension, diabetes or dyslipidemia is also preventive: immediate treatment burdens must be balanced against delayed benefits (p157, p164).":["u11"],"copy|Intensive glycemic treatment immediately increases serious hypoglycemia risk; antihypertensive initiation appears to increase hip-fracture risk in the first 45 days (p164).":["u12"],"copy|Immunization has a more favorable near-term risk–benefit balance than many screening interventions; the chapter emphasizes influenza, pneumococcal and zoster vaccination (p165).":["u13"],"copy|Support healthy behaviors and smoking cessation; maintaining behavior change requires the patient's participation (p165, p168).":["u14"],"copy|Detecting more disease is not the same as producing net benefit during the patient's lifetime; timing of harms and benefits matters (p157, p164).":["u15"],"copy|For nursing-home residents or serious illness, prioritize near-term harms, competing needs and time-to-benefit rather than simply continuing routine preventive care (p169).":["u16"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u17"],"copy|CHAPTER 11s / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 11 - Prevention and Screening; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|Decision framework":["u22"],"copy|Cancer screening":["u23"],"copy|Management & prevention":["u24"],"copy|Exam traps":["u25"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (2):":["u26"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (5):":["u26"],"copy|Fecal occult blood screening takes approximately 10 years to prevent 1 colorectal-cancer death per 1000 screened. Serious harm comes chiefly from downstream procedures, including bleeding or perforation during colonoscopy (p161).":["u7"],"copy|Flexible sigmoidoscopy has a similar estimated time to benefit (p161). In the later COLONPREV trial of adults aged 50–69, invitation to biennial FIT was noninferior to invitation to one-time colonoscopy for colorectal cancer mortality at ten years. That comparison does not give a time to benefit versus no screening. COLONPREV, 2025.":["u8"],"copy|Cervix. At age 65, check the actual screening history. The chapter defines adequate prior screening as three consecutive negative cytology results or two consecutive negative HPV co-tests in the preceding ten years, with the most recent within five years. A healthy older woman without adequate prior screening may still benefit. Consider whether she could undergo treatment if a lesion is found. False positives can lead to colposcopy and biopsy (p163).":["u37"],"copy|Flexible sigmoidoscopy has a similar estimated time to benefit (p161). Current practice (not the book): In the later COLONPREV trial of adults aged 50–69, invitation to biennial FIT was noninferior to invitation to one-time colonoscopy for colorectal cancer mortality at ten years. That comparison does not give a time to benefit versus no screening. COLONPREV, 2025.":["u8"],"copy|The model found biennial mammography cost-effective with life expectancy of at least 9.5 years (p161).":["u60"],"copy|Table 11-2 gives 1–5 years for hypertension treatment and 3 months–2.5 years for statins, from first detectable to near-maximal population benefit.":["u163"],"copy|The accompanying prose gives a shorter estimate of under 3 months for statins after a recent myocardial infarction, and several years for primary prevention.":["u164"],"copy|Orthostasis and falls can occur soon after starting blood pressure treatment.":["u80"],"copy|CHAPTER 11 / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|June 2023 Q85":["u18"],"copy|September 2024 Q92":["u19"]},"maxId":164,"sequenceOffsets":[[165,-138],[175,-137],[197,-136],[216,-134],[297,0]]}
